! >>> LINES 3 AND 10 PRINT "C0" WHERE THE PAGE ITSELF SAYS "00". <<<
! The page carries two bare B rows and three BD,$ rows.  Line 3
! (B,T N2) and line 10 (BD,$) print an opcode byte whose first glyph
! is unmistakably a round, wide-mouthed C at 1800 dpi; line 20
! (B,T OK) and lines 12 and 23 (BD,$) print two narrow closed ovals,
! "00", just as unmistakably.  Identical statements cannot assemble
! to different opcode bytes, so one of each pair is ink -- and the
! majority on this page plus the corpus say which: printed p.89 line
! 29's BD,$ prints 005075.04 00, and a bare B prints ".x0 00" on
! forty-nine independently transcribed rows across printed pp.44-80
! (printed p.88 lines 28/42/46/54 among them).  THE !CHECK ROWS BELOW
! THEREFORE READ 00 ON LINES 3 AND 10, and the discrepancy is
! recorded here rather than smoothed away.  (The header's note on
! lines 49/53 settles the same question a cheaper way: this page
! prints C's inside OCTAL fields, where a C cannot occur, so the
! open glyph is demonstrably a 0 on this page's own ink.)  THE DIRECTION OF THE
! ARGUMENT MATTERS: a worn drum and a dry ribbon can only OPEN a
! glyph, never close one, so an observed C may be a damaged 0 while
! an observed 0 can never be a damaged C.  Where one statement
! prints both ways on one page, the CLOSED reading is the true one.
! The contrast that keeps
! this honest is line 1: BAE really does print C2, and so does every
! BAE on printed p.88.  The C is ink only where the same statement's
! twin on the same page prints a closed glyph.

! ---- CHECKS, read by tests/verify_mcp_page.py 95 -----------------
